      <title>PC shipments fall 20.1 percent in “sharpest decline” since Q1 2023</title>
      <author>
                    Scharon Harding
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/10/pc-shipments-fall-20-1-percent-in-sharpest-decline-since-q1-2023/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/10/pc-shipments-fall-20-1-percent-in-sharpest-decline-since-q1-2023/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">4a3580b054c0a2bb4d29ece93826af3234341aca</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:54:52 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         &amp;#8220;The current decline may be just the beginning of a new downward cycle.&amp;#8221;       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1024" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2298480400-1024x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="The Microsoft Surface Laptop Ultra during an event in San Francisco, California, US, on Wednesday, Oct. 7, 2026." loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;       The Microsoft Surface Laptop Ultra during an event in San Francisco, California, US, on Wednesday, Oct. 7, 2026.                 &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                        &lt;a class="caption-credit-link text-gray-400 no-underline hover:text-gray-500" href="https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/the-microsoft-surface-laptop-ultra-during-an-event-in-san-news-photo/2298480400"               target="_blank"&gt;                       David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images                        &lt;/a&gt;                   &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;p&gt;PC shipments saw a steep drop of about 20 percent in Q3 2026, analysts reported this week.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The industry saw its “sharpest decline since “Q1 2023,” Omdia said in an announcement toady. Global shipments of laptops, desktops, and workstations fell 21.2 percent year over year (YoY) to 58.1 million devices, the analyst said.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Desktop PC shipments (including desktop workstations) decreased 23.5 percent to 11.7 million units, and laptop shipments (including laptop workstations) declined 20.6 percent to 46.4 million, per Omdia.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23.png"&gt;&lt;div class="ars-lightbox"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="640" height="360" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-640x360.png" class="right medium" alt="Omdia Global PC shipments Q3 2026 bar graph" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-640x360.png 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-1024x576.png 1024w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-768x432.png 768w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-384x216.png 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-1152x648.png 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23-980x551.png 980w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/In-3Q26-Global-PC-shipments-faced-sharpest-decline-since-1Q23.png 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                                              &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                    Credit:                                       Omdia                                   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt;IDC shared similar findings. The analyst said this week that PC shipments fell 20.1 percent YoY to 62.7 million units, compared to 78.5 million in Q3 2025.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The industry also shipped 9.1 percent fewer PCs in Q3 2026 than it did in Q2 2026, when 68.2 million units shipped, per IDC.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The 20.1 percent YoY decline is the biggest drop that the IDC has ever recorded during a year’s third quarter, the IDC said, per &lt;a href="https://www.pcmag.com/news/with-20-dip-q3-was-a-historically-bad-quarter-for-pc-shipments"&gt;PCMag&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The drop in shipments is also notable considering that Q3 PC shipments usually surpass Q2’s numbers.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“If looking at all quarters, then we have seen other quarters in recent years with larger declines, such as 2022 Q4, which was -28.2 percent, and 2023 Q1, which was -28.7 percent. Both were coming off pandemic highs,” Jitesh Ubrani, research director for consumer devices at IDC, told PCMag.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;In its announcement this week, IDC said that many PC vendors and sales channels stocked up on PC shipments earlier in the year in anticipation of memory price hikes. That meant minimal demand remained during Q3.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“[Sales] channels are now worried about carrying too much inventory into a market where high prices are suppressing demand,” Ubrani said in a statement.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;“That could translate into promotions and some short-term relief for consumers, but we don’t expect pricing anywhere near what it was a year ago. Prices will remain elevated. With macro conditions worsening, the risk is that the outlook for the next few quarters gets worse before it gets better.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image_63283a.jpg"&gt;&lt;div class="ars-lightbox"&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="640" height="384" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image_63283a-640x384.jpg" class="right medium" alt="Wordwide PC Shipments and Growth, 2026Q3 bar graph" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image_63283a-640x384.jpg 640w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/image_63283a.jpg 690w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 640px) 100vw, 640px"&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                                              &lt;div class="ars-gallery-caption-credit"&gt;Credit:                    Credit:                                       IDC                                   &lt;/div&gt;                           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;/div&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;IDC said that it expects PC prices to decrease “only modestly and briefly.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Omdia research director Ishan Dutt said the Q3 drop “comes as no surprise.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“The impact of upstream component cost increases has now fully worked its way through to end-market pricing, with consumers and IT decision-makers responding accordingly. On the supply side, vendors and their ODM partners began scaling back production in [Q3], while channel partners continued working through elevated inventory levels,” he said in a statement.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Since 2025, surging demand for HBM and server DRAM for AI workloads has led to limited manufacturing capacity for consumer devices, like consumer PCs, &lt;a href="https://www.idc.com/resource-center/blog/higher-asps-lower-unit-volumes-how-the-memory-crisis-is-reshaping-the-pc-and-smartphone-outlook/"&gt;smartphones,&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/05/the-nintendo-switch-2-is-getting-more-expensive-later-this-year/"&gt;gaming consoles&lt;/a&gt;. Memory &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2025/12/for-just-a-couple-of-months-in-the-middle-of-2025-it-was-an-ok-time-to-build-a-pc/"&gt;and storage&lt;/a&gt; now &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/02/ram-now-represents-35-percent-of-bill-of-materials-for-hp-pcs/"&gt;represent nearly 40 percent of the bill of materials &lt;/a&gt;for PC manufacturing, but it’s typically 15 percent, per Ben Yeh, principal analyst at Omdia. Meanwhile, PC prices have increased “more than fourfold,” he said, adding:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Although PC vendors have raised prices multiple times over the past few quarters, these increases have yet to fully offset the impact. These pressures are expected to persist, and the current decline may be just the beginning of a new downward cycle.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;p&gt;Omdia expects PC shipments to decline 24 percent YoY next quarter and then 7 percent in 2027.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Last week, executives from Micron and Samsung indicated the components shortages could &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/10/memory-supplies-are-only-getting-tighter-micron-ceo-says/"&gt;continue through 2028&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;“Alongside continued increases in memory and storage prices, shortages of processors, graphics cards, and numerous IC components have made supply chain and inventory management more difficult for PC vendors,” Omdia said.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <category>desktops</category>
      <category>idc</category>
      <category>laptops</category>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AI disqualification yields new Nikon Small World in Motion winner</title>
      <author>
                    Jennifer Ouellette
                </author>
      <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/</link>
      <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/#comments</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">3fd143dce3daea832cd976b9c76d2f09028837d6</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:44:06 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;         New winner is Nguyen Nam Nhat of Vietnam for video of a roundworm and single-celled Dileptus.       &lt;/p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img width="1152" height="648" src="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-1152x648.jpg" class="intro-image absolute min-w-full min-h-full h-auto object-cover" alt="A tiny roundworm and a single-celled Dileptus explore their microscopic world" loading="eager" decoding="async" fetchpriority="high" srcset="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-1152x648.jpg 1152w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-384x216.jpg 384w, https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-768x432.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1152px) 100vw, 1152px" /&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;                       &lt;span class="caption-credit mt-2 text-xs"&gt;           Credit:                       Nguyen Nam Nhat                    &lt;/span&gt;           &lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;                                                         &lt;figure class="ars-video ars-video--horizontal"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;div class="relative"&gt;&lt;a href="https://player.vimeo.com/video/1234474920?color=ff9933"&gt;https://player.vimeo.com/video/1234474920?color=ff9933&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="caption font-impact dusk:text-gray-300 mb-4 mt-2 inline-flex flex-row items-stretch gap-1 text-base leading-tight text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-300"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-icon bg-[left_top_5px] w-[10px] shrink-0"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-content"&gt;       Credit: Nguyen Nam Nhat.            &lt;/div&gt;   &lt;/div&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;/figure&gt; &lt;p&gt;Last month &lt;a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/09/meet-the-winner-of-nikons-small-world-in-motion-video-contest/"&gt;we covered&lt;/a&gt; the winner of Nikon’s Small World in Motion video: Ning Xu of Tsinghua University in China, whose video captured tiny cilia beating in the airways of a child with a rare respiratory disorder. But &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/update-october-9-an-update-from-nikon-small-ugcPost-7506310017814401024-QfgZ/"&gt;allegations surfaced&lt;/a&gt; about Xu’s &lt;a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ck4gjn1yzprno"&gt;use of AI&lt;/a&gt; while making the video. Nikon launched an investigation and concluded that Xu’s entry did not comply with competition rules. The new winner is Nguyen Nam Nhat of Vietnam with a video of a tiny roundworm and a single-celled organism (above).&lt;/p&gt; &lt;div&gt; &lt;p data-path-to-node="3"&gt;According to a &lt;a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ck4gjn1yzprno"&gt;BBC report,&lt;/a&gt; some researchers expressed concern that Xu’s video had structural abnormalities and that cell features appeared and disappeared in a way one would not see in actual cellular biology. Others spotted what looked like AI watermarks embedded within the source files. Xu said he used AI “to distinguish and visualize features in the reconstructed grayscale images”; however, he denied that AI was used “to generate the experimental movie, the cilia, or their motion.”&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;p&gt;Founded in 2011, the Nikon Small World in Motion competition is a spinoff of the company’s Small World Photomicrography Competition, which has been around since 1974. Both are meant “to showcase the beauty and complexity of things seen through the light microscope”—one through still photographs, the other through photovideography, taking advantage of all the technological advances that now allow users to make movies or time-lapse images through the microscope.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The other winners also moved up a notch with Xu’s disqualification. Benedikt Pleyer of Bavaria, Germany, placed second with a video of jellyfish larvae suspended in water droplets; Andrew Moore of Howard Hughes Medical Institute in the US placed third with a video of synchronized cell division in neighboring cells; Patrick Hickey of Hypha Research Ltd. in Edinburgh, Scotland, placed fourth with a video of the dynamics of mitochondria and chloroplasts in Turtle Vine leaf cells; and Igor Adameyko placed fifth with a video of a tiny, colorful sea slug (&lt;i&gt;Cyerce kikutarobabai&lt;/i&gt;) on the move.&lt;/p&gt;                                                                             &lt;p&gt;Here is Nikon’s full statement:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;After thoroughly re-evaluating the video and supporting materials and consulting with members of our judging panel, Nikon has determined that the winning video did not comply with the competition rules regarding generative AI. Nikon has adjusted the competition rankings on our website to reflect this decision.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This decision is based solely on the submitted video’s eligibility under the rules outlined for this competition. It should not be interpreted as a judgment of the entrant’s professional reputation, scientific contributions, or intent.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Advances in imaging and artificial intelligence continue to evolve rapidly, creating new challenges for organizations across many fields. This event has shown the need for Nikon Small World and Nikon Small World in Motion to revisit rules and evaluation procedures for future competition entries.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The Nikon Small World and Nikon Small World in Motion competitions celebrate excellence in imaging, both scientific and artistic in nature. We value the integrity of each image and video selected to appear in our winning galleries, and remain committed to upholding the standards of excellence that have defined this competition.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;</description>
      <category>nikon-small-world-in-motion-video</category>
    </item>
